<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pham Van Dong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/2000">2000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Phạm Văn Đồng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pham Van Dong was a Vietnamese communist leader and close associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> delegation at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Conference of 1954 and was prime minister of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from 1955 and of reunified Vietnam until 1987, one of the longest-serving heads of government of the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pham-van-dong.jpg" alt="Pham Van Dong" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ph%E1%BA%A1m_V%C4%83n_%C4%90%E1%BB%93ng_1972.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Phạm Văn Đồng was born on 1 March 1906, according to an official report, into a family of civil servants in Quảng Ngãi Province on the central coast. In 1925 he joined fellow students in a sit-in mourning the patriotic scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phan-chau-trinh">Phan Chu Trinh</a>. In 1926 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> for a training course run by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Nguyen Ai Quoc</a>, later Ho Chi Minh, and joined the Vietnamese Revolutionary Youth League. Arrested in 1929, he was sentenced by the French colonial authorities to ten years and served on Poulo Condor Island until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a>'s amnesty of 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1940 he went secretly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with Võ Nguyên Giáp, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a> and was tasked with building a base at the border. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution">August Revolution</a> of 1945 he became minister of finance of the new Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and in 1946 headed its delegation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>, where talks failed because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> set no firm date for a referendum in Cochinchina. He became Deputy Prime Minister in July 1949. In May 1954 he led the delegation to the Geneva Conference on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> and signed the accords with the French premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-mendes-france">Pierre Mendès France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Đồng was minister of foreign affairs from September 1954 and prime minister from 1955 until he retired in 1987, over thirty years. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>'s strokes in the early 1960s he became the face of North Vietnam in the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, speaking to foreign diplomats and journalists.</p><p>On 8 June 1964 he met the Canadian diplomat J. Blair Seaborn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, who carried an offer from President Johnson of aid and recognition in exchange for an end to attempts to overthrow the South Vietnamese government, with a warning of bombing. Đồng called the terms unacceptable and demanded an end of American assistance to the south, its neutrality and a coalition government including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a>. He died in Hanoi on 29 April 2000, aged 94.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phạm Văn Đồng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After meeting Jawaharlal Nehru and U Nu in 1954 he turned towards nonalignment rather than the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization, and at the Bandung Conference in April 1955 Zhou Enlai and <b>Phạm Văn Đồng</b> assured him that their countries would respect Cambodia's independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1976 the Fourth Party Congress renamed the ruling party the Communist Party of Vietnam and confirmed Le Duan as general secretary, with <b>Pham Van Dong</b> as prime minister and Ton Duc Thang as president.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Quảng Ngãi, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15,108.67&amp;year=1967" aria-label="Quảng Ngãi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7519 1606 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pham Van Dong"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7969 1876h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7969 1876h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15,108.67&amp;year=1967">What was here in 1967? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
